[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"System Design Tutorial: Understanding NoSQL Database Paradigms"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern distributed systems require data storage solutions that scale horizontally, handle unstructured or semi-structured data, and provide high availability. Unlike traditional Relational Database Management Systems (RDBMS) that rely on strict ACID transactions and rigid schemas, NoSQL databases offer diverse data models tailored to specific access patterns."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Key-Value Stores"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Key-Value stores are the simplest NoSQL paradigm. They operate like a massive distributed hash table where data is retrieved using a unique key. The value is treated as an opaque blob by the database engine."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Cases: Session management, caching layers (e.g., Redis), shopping carts, and real-time recommendation profiles."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strengths: Extremely high throughput, predictable O(1) read/write latency, and effortless horizontal partitioning (sharding)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Weaknesses: Lack of query capabilities on values; inefficient for searching or filtering by attributes inside the value."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"SET user:1001 "{"name": "Alice", "status": "active"}"
GET user:1001"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Document Databases"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Document databases store data in semi-structured formats like JSON, BSON, or XML. Unlike Key-Value stores, the database understands the internal structure of the document, allowing indexing and querying on nested fields."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Cases: Content management systems, blogging platforms, catalogs, and user profiles with varying attributes."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strengths: Flexible schemas, rich query languages, and intuitive mapping to object-oriented application code."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Weaknesses: Complex multi-document transactions can be challenging, and unconstrained nesting can lead to large document sizes."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"db.users.insertOne({
  "userId": 2002,
  "profile": {
    "name": "Bob",
    "interests": ["hiking", "coding"]
  }
})"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Column-Family Data Stores"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Column-Family stores (often called Wide-Column stores) organize data into column families rather than rows. Instead of storing a whole row contiguously, columns of a single family are stored together on disk. This is optimized for high read/write volumes over massive datasets."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Cases: Time-series data, Internet of Things (IoT) sensor logging, event tracking, and messaging systems."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strengths: High write scalability, excellent compression ratios, and fast aggregations over specific column ranges."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Weaknesses: Requires careful upfront query-driven design; poorly suited for ad-hoc querying or complex joins."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"CREATE KEYSPACE analytics WITH replication = {'class': 'SimpleStrategy', 'replication_factor': 3};
CREATE TABLE analytics.events (device_id text, event_time timestamp, payload text, PRIMARY KEY (device_id, event_time));"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Graph Databases"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Graph databases utilize nodes, edges, and properties to represent and store data. They are specifically designed for data whose relationships are as important as, or more important than, the data itself."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Cases: Social networks, fraud detection, recommendation engines, and network topology mapping."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strengths: Exceptional performance for traversing deep, multi-level relationships without expensive JOIN operations."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Weaknesses: Difficult to scale horizontally across multiple machines while maintaining low-latency graph traversals."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"MATCH (p:Person {name: "Alice"})-[:FRIENDS_WITH]->(friend)
RETURN friend.name"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and System Design Trade-offs"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right NoSQL paradigm depends heavily on your access patterns rather than your data shapes. Always analyze your read-to-write ratio, scalability requirements, and query complexity before finalizing your system architecture."}]
